= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The repeated, similarly oriented rounded triangular outlines in panel (a) are characteristic of <AFM tip convolution>: a narrow raised object can image the shape of a blunt or contaminated <AFM probe>, rather than reveal its own lateral shape. A finite tip radius broadens lateral dimensions. Replace or clean the <AFM probe>, check it on an independently characterized sharp-feature standard, and repeat at low load; agreement after a tip change is stronger evidence than a visually pleasing image. Image processing cannot uniquely recover unmeasured sidewalls or prove that every particle with a similar outline is an artefact.

Panel (b) contains horizontal streaks associated with steep features. The natural instrumental diagnosis is <AFM feedback artefact>: the height controller does not track a rapid change faithfully, or rings after it. Reduce the scan speed and adjust proportional/integral gains below the oscillation threshold. Compare trace and retrace and rotate the fast-scan direction: a streak following the scan rather than the specimen is instrumental. Loose contamination can also produce scan-line streaks, so clean or change the <AFM probe> if tuning does not remove them. **Tip-shape broadening in (a), and scan-direction tracking/streaking in (b), are the principal diagnoses; the images alone do not uniquely establish every underlying cause.**

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<piezoelectric scanner creep> is the continued slow displacement after a step in scanner drive, even when the commanded voltage has stopped changing. The scanner's <strain> continues to relax, and the motion can resemble a slowly decaying, approximately logarithmic drift. After changing scan size, position or offset, early lines can therefore have stretched or compressed coordinates. Allow settling, avoid large abrupt moves, use a position-sensor <closed-loop control> system, and compare repeated images. This time-dependent response differs from the direction-dependent <piezoelectric hysteresis> and from ordinary thermal drift.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**A steady-current image is not supported by the electrical connection drawn.** <tunnelling atomic force microscopy> requires a conducting <AFM probe>, an applied <bias voltage> and a continuous return path from the contacted region to the current amplifier. <graphene> is conducting, but a flake on insulating <glass> is not electrically grounded merely because the other side or edge of the <glass> is earthed. Charging transients or displacement current are not its steady conductivity. Add a metal contact actually touching the <graphene> before using its current contrast; ordinary force-based <surface topography> can still be recorded without that contact. The manufacturer's nanostructure measurements explicitly distinguish objects connected to conducting pads from unconnected particles on the same insulating substrate.<Simultaneous Electrical and Mechanical Property Mapping at the Nanoscale with PeakForce TUNA by Bruker>[application note 132, Figure 11 and accompanying discussion of electrical connection]{ref}

= b
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**<quantitative nanomechanical mapping> can image the flake without an electrical return path.** Repeated <AFM force curves> provide <surface topography>, <adhesion> and deformation or effective <elastic modulus> channels. The step and differences in tip–surface interaction can distinguish <graphene> from <glass>. For an atomically thin supported sheet the contact response is strongly substrate dependent, so fitting a three-dimensional half-space contact model does not directly measure the intrinsic in-plane <Young's modulus> of free <graphene>. Calibrate the <cantilever spring constant>, deflection sensitivity and tip radius, use small loads, and compare bare substrate. The manufacturer's description specifies the force-curve origin of the mechanical channels.<Simultaneous Electrical and Mechanical Property Mapping at the Nanoscale with PeakForce TUNA by Bruker>[application note 132, Quantitative Mechanical Property Mapping]{ref}

= c
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**<friction force microscopy> is suitable.** Contact scanning twists the <AFM cantilever>; the lateral photodetector difference measures <friction> and can distinguish the supported <graphene> and <glass> even if their heights are close. Acquire both trace and retrace and use half their lateral-signal difference to suppress slope-induced cross-talk. Keep the normal load low enough to avoid dragging or damaging the flake, control humidity and contamination, and calibrate torsional sensitivity if a quantitative <coefficient of friction> is required. An electrical connection is unnecessary.

= d
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**Conventional <semiconductor> <scanning capacitance microscopy> is not a reliable dopant-contrast image of this specimen as drawn.** Its usual $dC/dV$ signal relies on a tip–insulator–<semiconductor> <capacitance> whose depletion region changes under AC bias. An isolated conducting <graphene> sheet on thick insulating <glass> provides neither that conventional depletion geometry nor a controlled charge reservoir; simply earthing <glass> does not supply one. Capacitive or electrostatic contrast may be detectable with a suitably designed back <electrode>, a contacted flake and an appropriate capacitance-sensitive instrument, but that is an additional electrical device specification. Force-based <surface topography> remains possible independently of the specialized <capacitance> channel.

= 2
{parent=Section A(i)}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

At position (1), the <AFM probe> is far from the surface, with approximately zero <force>. At (2), attractive interactions bend the <AFM cantilever> toward the surface; when the attractive <force> gradient exceeds the <cantilever spring constant>, the equilibrium becomes unstable and <jump-to-contact> occurs. Position (3) is on the mechanical-contact branch: changes in imposed height largely bend the <AFM cantilever> and, for a compliant specimen, indent the sample. This branch can include compressive contact and adhesive contact under tension; contact does not itself imply a positive <force> at every point. At (4), during withdrawal the tip remains attached and pulls the <AFM cantilever> downward until the <pull-off force> is reached and it detaches. **Approach and retraction follow different paths because <adhesion> creates a hysteretic contact.** The numbered points identify stages on the source curve, not four extra question parts.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In <quantitative nanomechanical mapping>, the operational <adhesion> is the magnitude of the most attractive <force> immediately before detachment on a calibrated retraction curve:
$$
F_{\rm adh}=-\min F_{\rm retract},\qquad F=k_c\delta_c.
$$
Here $k_c$ is the <cantilever spring constant> and $\delta_c$ its deflection after baseline subtraction. The area between approach and retraction relates to dissipated work, whereas the minimum <force> is a <force>, not an <energy>. Chemical attraction, <Van der Waals forces> and ambient-water capillary bridges can all contribute. Match loading rate and peak load, calibrate deflection sensitivity and $k_c$, compare clean reference surfaces, and control humidity; otherwise different maps may reflect different tips or capillary conditions rather than different specimens.

\Image[paper-1-afm-contact.png]
{title=Original illustrative force-cycle schematic distinguishing pull-off force from dissipated work}

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Three suitable approaches give complementary contrast. <tapping-mode atomic force microscopy> supplies <surface topography> and an oscillation-phase image. It reduces lateral drag and is often effective for soft <block copolymer> domains. Its phase contrast depends on elastic response, <adhesion>, dissipation and operating setpoint together: a bright phase domain is not automatically the stiffer polymer. Check contrast as amplitude and setpoint change and use a known reference.

<quantitative nanomechanical mapping> fits the repulsive portions of many <AFM force curves> and separately maps effective <elastic modulus>, <adhesion> and deformation. It directly addresses the stiffness difference and limits maximum <force>. Its quantitative result depends on tip radius, <force> calibration, contact model, loading rate and film thickness; domains narrower than the contact footprint are mixed and the substrate can dominate a thin film. Use small indentation, substrate controls and a suitable viscoelastic interpretation.

<friction force microscopy> can reveal domains with different frictional and adhesive response, using torsional deflection during contact scans. It is simple and sensitive to surface chemistry, but continuous shear may deform or displace soft domains, and slope/topography can contaminate the lateral signal. Acquire trace–retrace pairs at several low normal loads and check reproducibility. **Use a mechanical-modulus map to test the stiffness assignment; phase or <friction> contrast alone does not uniquely identify stiffness or chemical composition.**

