= Solution
{parent=iii}

In the high-surface-potential <Schulze-Hardy rule>, the <critical coagulation concentration> scales approximately as $z^{-6}$ with the <counterion> valence. Hence
$$
c_{\rm Al}=c_{\rm Ca}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^6
=0.01\frac{64}{729}
=\boxed{8.78\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}}.
$$
The more highly charged <counterion> therefore requires about eleven times less <concentration> in this idealized estimate. This assumes the cations are the <counterions> of a negatively charged surface, the surface-potential regime is appropriate, and the only changing mechanism is electrostatic stabilization. Specific <adsorption> and hydrolysis of aluminium <ions> can invalidate this scaling; matching <ionic strengths> instead is not the same prediction.

= 6
{parent=Section A(iii)}
{scope}

= i
{parent=6}
{scope}

= a
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The more negative standard <reduction> potential identifies iron as the species more readily oxidized. Combining $\mathrm{Fe\to Fe^{2+}+2e^-}$ with $\mathrm{Sn^{2+}+2e^-\to Sn}$ gives the spontaneous standard <galvanic cell> reaction
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{Fe(s)+Sn^{2+}(aq)\longrightarrow Fe^{2+}(aq)+Sn(s)}}.
$$
The <electron> balance is two per reaction, and the activities of the pure metals are unity.

= b
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <galvanic cell>, subtract the standard <anode> <reduction> potential from the standard <cathode> <reduction> potential:
$$
E^\circ_{\rm cell}=E^\circ_{\rm Sn^{2+}/Sn}-E^\circ_{\rm Fe^{2+}/Fe}
=-0.136-(-0.440)=\boxed{+0.304\ \mathrm V}.
$$
A <reduction> potential is not multiplied by the number of transferred <electrons>.

= c
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**Iron is the negative <anode> and tin is the positive <cathode>** of this spontaneous <galvanic cell>. <electrons> travel through the external circuit from iron to tin. An intact tin coating protects iron mainly by physically excluding the corrosive medium. If a defect exposes iron while the metals remain electrically connected, tin is not a sacrificial coating: the exposed iron can become the anodic corrosion site. A small exposed iron area coupled to a large cathodic tin area can be especially damaging; this distinction follows from the electrode-potential order rather than the appearance of the coating.

= ii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

With $n=2$, the standard reaction <Gibbs free energy> and dimensionless <equilibrium constant> are
$$
\Delta_rG^\circ=-nFE^\circ=-2(96500)(0.304)
=\boxed{-58.672\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}},
\qquad
K=\exp\!\left(\frac{nFE^\circ}{RT}\right)
=\boxed{1.93\times10^{10}}.
$$
For the reaction as written, $Q=a_{\rm Fe^{2+}}/a_{\rm Sn^{2+}}$, and the <Nernst equation> gives $E=E^\circ-(RT/2F)\ln Q$. Consequently **zero cell potential requires $[\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}]/[\mathrm{Sn^{2+}}]=K\simeq1.93\times10^{10}$** under the specified unit-activity-coefficient assumption. Reversing that ratio reverses the conclusion. This mathematical ratio does not by itself guarantee experimentally attainable <concentrations> without precipitation or other chemistry.

= 7
{parent=Section A(iii)}
{scope}

= i
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <Ragone plot> has <specific energy> $e_s=E/m$ on its horizontal logarithmic axis and <specific power> $p_s=P/m$ on its vertical logarithmic axis, normally in $\mathrm{Wh\,kg^{-1}}$ and $\mathrm{W\,kg^{-1}}$. The comparison must use the same <mass> convention: complete devices cannot fairly be compared with electrode-active-material values. At approximately constant delivered power, the discharge time is
$$
t_{\rm h}=\frac{e_s}{p_s},\qquad t_{\rm s}=3600\frac{e_s}{p_s}.
$$
Thus constant-time lines slope upward on the log–log plot. **Ordinary <capacitors> lie toward high power and low <energy>; <batteries> toward higher <energy> and lower power; <supercapacitors> bridge the two.** Technology and operating limits produce overlapping regions, not universal sharp boxes.

\Image[paper-1-ragone.png]
{title=Original qualitative Ragone comparison with logarithmic axes and representative constant-discharge-time lines}

= ii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

An ordinary <capacitor> stores <energy> by separating charge across a <dielectric>: $E=CV^2/2$ and $Q=CV$. Electronic charge redistribution is rapid, giving high <specific power>, but the <dielectric> breakdown field and modest <capacitance> limit <specific energy>. Series resistance limits pulse power and produces heating; a large nominal <capacitance> does not remove those losses.

A <supercapacitor> has a much larger interfacial area and nanometre-scale charge separation. An <electric double-layer capacitor> stores charge without a bulk <redox> transformation; <pseudocapacitance> adds sufficiently fast surface or near-surface <redox> storage. <ion> motion in pores, <electrolyte> stability and series resistance constrain its response. Its <specific energy> usually exceeds an ordinary <capacitor>'s while high <specific power> and cycling durability remain possible.

An <electric battery> stores chemical <free energy> through coupled <electrode> <oxidation-reduction reactions>. It can store substantial <energy> per <mass>, but charge transfer, ionic transport, <diffusion> and heat removal constrain rapid discharge. The terminal voltage and accessible capacity depend on discharge rate and state of charge. Accordingly the plotted order follows the storage and transport mechanisms; it is not a claim that every <battery> has less power than every <supercapacitor>.

